course: world
lesson: 109
title: "Five more minutes" and other waiting games
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: "Five more minutes!" Kai asks at 8:00 and finally stops at 8:25, when Dad pulls the plug: 25 minutes, five lots of five. People mean their promise, then lose track while they play. Mum is no better: "Leaving in 10 minutes," then 25 minutes of chatting at the door. "Classic," says Esme.
explain: Many waiting words are stretchy, not exact: "in a bit", "back in a jiffy", "a tick", "a mo", "two shakes". In South Africa "now now" means soon, maybe in a while, and "just now" can even mean later. A text saying "omw" (on my way) tells you nothing about how far away someone is; ask "how many minutes?" instead.
explain: Some waits can be worked out. In a queue, multiply: 4 people in front, about 3 minutes each, is 12 minutes. A theme park posts its wait time, 45 minutes, so you can choose another ride.
explain: Live signs and apps guess from where the bus is, and traffic changes the guess: "Next bus 4 min" can still say "2 min" four minutes later. Doctors run late too, because some patients need more time than planned. So bring a book, and set a timer for your "five more minutes".
fact: From 8:00 to 8:25 is 25 minutes, which is 5 lots of 5 minutes.
fact: People often say a short time and take longer; Mum's "10 minutes" that became 25 was 15 minutes longer.
fact: A queue wait can be worked out by multiplying the people in front by the time each takes: 4 × 3 = 12 minutes.
fact: A 45-minute wait is 15 minutes less than an hour.
fact: 2 minutes is 120 seconds; a watched toaster only seems slow because you are staring at it.
fact: Being patient means waiting calmly without fussing; it is not an amount of time.
fact: "A watched pot never boils" is a saying meaning time feels slower when you stare and wait.
fact: Doctors often run late because some patients need more time than planned; arriving at 10:00 for 10:10 and being called at 10:35 means 25 minutes after the booked time.
fact: Asking every 5 minutes for 30 minutes means asking 6 times, because 30 ÷ 5 = 6.
fact: Live bus signs are guesses that change with traffic: "4 min" that becomes "2 min" after 4 minutes means a 6-minute wait in total.
fact: "A jiffy", "a tick", "a mo" and "two shakes" all mean a short moment, but nobody measures them; "back in a jiffy" once took 12 minutes.
fact: In South African English "now" can mean soon, "now now" a bit sooner, and "just now" a bit later.
fact: "omw" in a text means "on my way"; it does not say how many minutes away someone is.
fact: Words people use: "pull the plug" means switch it off, "classic" means typical, "ain't" means isn't, "you're having a laugh" means you must be joking (UK), "lekker" means great (South Africa) and "lah" is added to the end of sentences in Singapore.
exercise: Kai asks for five more minutes at 8:00 and stops at 8:25. How long did he really play on? | 25 minutes | 5 minutes | 20 minutes | 30 minutes
exercise: Mum says "leaving in 10 minutes" and chats for 25. How much longer than she said? | 15 minutes | 25 minutes | 35 minutes | 10 minutes
exercise: Four people are in front of Nathan, each taking about 3 minutes. How long until his turn? | 12 minutes | 7 minutes | 15 minutes | 4 minutes
exercise: The theme park sign says "Wait time 45 mins". How much less than an hour is that? | 15 minutes | 45 minutes | 25 minutes | 5 minutes
exercise: Tia's toaster takes 2 minutes. How many seconds is that? | 120 | 200 | 20 | 60
exercise: What does "a watched pot never boils" mean? | Time feels slower when you stare and wait | Pots do not boil if anyone looks right at them | Water boils faster when nobody is at home | Cooking should never, ever be watched by kids
exercise: Jake arrives at 10:00 for a 10:10 appointment and is called at 10:35. How long after the booked time is that? | 25 minutes | 35 minutes | 10 minutes | 45 minutes
exercise: Why do doctors often run late? | Some patients need more time than planned | Doctors start every single day an hour late | Waiting rooms make all the clocks run slow | Doctors always see the youngest patients first
exercise: Phoebe asks to go to the park every 5 minutes for 30 minutes. How many times does she ask? | 6 | 5 | 30 | 35
exercise: The sign says "Next bus 4 min". Four minutes later it says "2 min". How long will Rhys have waited in total? | 6 minutes | 2 minutes | 4 minutes | 8 minutes
exercise: Ana's dad says "back in a jiffy" and comes out 12 minutes later. What is a jiffy in everyday talk? | A short moment, not measured | Exactly 12 minutes, by the clock | Exactly one second | One hour
exercise: Elijah's grandma in South Africa says, "I'll do it now now." What does she mean? | Soon, maybe in a while | This very second, right away | Tomorrow morning | Never
exercise: Isaac has waited 20 minutes; the film starts in 5; his mate is 10 minutes away. How much of the film will the mate miss? | 5 minutes | 10 minutes | 20 minutes | None of it
exercise: What does "omw" mean in a text? | On my way | Oh my word | Out my window | One more wait
exercise: Which is the best way to make "five more minutes" really five minutes? | Set a timer and stop when it rings | Promise really, really hard to stop | Ask Dad to guess when it's time to stop | Play twice as fast for five minutes
test: Zara's brother at the theme park: "Wait's 45 mins? Nah, I ain't standing here for an hour." Put him right. | It is 45 minutes, 15 less than an hour | He's right, 45 minutes is an hour | It is 45 seconds, not minutes | It is an hour and 45 minutes
test: Tia, staring at the toaster: "This is taking forever!" It takes 2 minutes. Why does it feel long? | Staring and waiting makes time feel slower | Toasters slow down when you watch them | 2 minutes is longer than it sounds, about 20 | The toaster has stopped working
test: Jake, fidgeting in the waiting room: "How long's patient?" What is the best answer? | Patient means waiting calmly, not an amount of time | Patient means exactly 10 minutes | Patient means until the doctor goes home | Patient means one hour at the doctor's
test: Phoebe asks every 5 minutes. Mum: "Ask once more and it's never." What would work better? | Ask once, then ask "what time?" | Ask every single minute instead, nicely | Ask Dad every 5 minutes instead of Mum | Cry and cry until Mum finally says yes
test: Rhys sees "Next bus 4 min", then, 4 minutes later, "Next bus 2 min". "You're having a laugh," he says. What's going on? | The sign guesses, and traffic changed the guess | The sign is broken and the bus has gone | Buses run backwards when they are late | The sign shows seconds, not minutes
test: A Joburg (Johannesburg, South Africa) cousin says, "Lekker, I'll fetch you just now." Should you be at the gate this second? | No, "just now" can mean a bit later in South Africa | Yes, "just now" always means this second | No, it means tomorrow | Yes, and she is already outside
test: Isaac texts, "where u at??" His mate: "omw". What's the most useful next text? | How many minutes away are you? | Are you on your way? | Where are you? | omw too
test: What would happen if a queue had 6 people in front of you, each taking about 3 minutes? | You would wait about 18 minutes | You would wait about 9 minutes | You would wait about 3 minutes | You would wait about 63 minutes
test: Esme rolls her eyes: "Classic, Mum said 10 and it's been 25." How much extra time was that? | 15 minutes | 35 minutes | 25 minutes | 2 and a half minutes
test: Kai, salty (annoyed) when Dad pulls the plug at 8:25: "It was only five minutes!" He asked at 8:00. What's true? | It was 25 minutes, five times as long | It was exactly five minutes | It was 8 minutes | It was 2 and a half minutes
test: In a cartoon, the hero says "back in a jiffy" and returns 100 years later. What is a jiffy really meant to be? | A short moment | Exactly 100 years | One day | One week
test: A Singapore aunt: "Wait, lah, 4 people in front, each 3 minutes only." How long is the wait? | 12 minutes | 7 minutes | 43 minutes | 1 minute
exam: It is given that four persons precede an individual in a queue, each requiring approximately three minutes. Determine the anticipated waiting period. | Twelve minutes | Seven minutes | Fifteen minutes | Four minutes
exam: Whereas a request for "five more minutes" is made at 8:00 and play ceases at 8:25, ascertain the actual duration of further play. | Twenty-five minutes | Five minutes exactly | Twenty minutes | Thirty minutes
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the expression "now now" in South African English. | It signifies "soon", possibly after some while | It signifies "at this precise instant" | It signifies "on the following day" | It signifies "never"
exam: Identify the principal reason for which medical appointments frequently commence later than scheduled. | Certain patients require more time than was planned | Physicians are obliged to commence one hour late | Waiting rooms cause clocks to run slowly | The youngest patients are always seen first
